WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.892 --> 00:00:18.834
next thing I remembered was waking up feeling as if I'd had a terrible nightmare.

2
00:00:19.854 --> 00:00:25.877
I became aware that someone was lifting me into a sitting position, and that this person was holding

3
00:00:25.937 --> 00:00:29.318
me more tenderly than I'd ever been held before.

4
00:00:30.479 --> 00:00:33.740
Soon I realized I was in my own bed; it was night.

5
00:00:34.864 --> 00:00:40.046
Bessie stood at the foot of the bed while a gentleman sat in a chair near my pillow

6
00:00:40.426 --> 00:00:41.447
and leaned over me.

7
00:00:42.547 --> 00:00:45.989
Turning from Bessie, I studied the face of the gentleman.

8
00:00:47.089 --> 00:00:47.809
It was Mr.

9
00:00:47.869 --> 00:00:51.331
Lloyd, an apothecary sometimes called by Mrs.

10
00:00:51.431 --> 00:00:53.232
Reed when the servants were ailing.

11
00:00:54.352 --> 00:00:57.593
For herself and the children she employed a physician.

12
00:00:57.613 --> 00:01:00.174
"Well, who am I?"

13
00:01:00.515 --> 00:01:01.135
he asked.

14
00:01:02.135 --> 00:01:05.429
"Mr. Lloyd," I said, offering him my hand.

15
00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:07.931
He took it and smiled.

16
00:01:07.951 --> 00:01:11.893
"We shall get along very well, Miss Jane Eyre."

17
00:01:13.675 --> 00:01:18.478
As he helped me lie down, he told Bessie that I was not to be disturbed during the

18
00:01:18.518 --> 00:01:25.442
night. He gave some more directions and said that he would return tomorrow, and then he departed.

19
00:01:26.563 --> 00:01:28.705
As he closed the door, my heart sank.

20
00:01:29.885 --> 00:01:33.428
I'd felt so cared for while he sat in the chair near my pillow.

21
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.641
"Would you like something to eat or drink?"

22
00:01:37.061 --> 00:01:38.301
Bessie asked kindly.

23
00:01:38.321 --> 00:01:40.502
"No, thank you, Bessie."

24
00:01:40.522 --> 00:01:46.023
"Then I think I shall go to bed, but you may call me if you want anything in

25
00:01:46.063 --> 00:01:46.543
the night."

26
00:01:47.704 --> 00:01:51.325
She was so wonderfully polite that I dared to ask a question.

27
00:01:51.345 --> 00:01:53.505
"What's the matter with me?

28
00:01:54.586 --> 00:01:55.186
Am I sick?"

29
00:01:55.206 --> 00:01:59.787
"You grew sick, I suppose, with crying in the red room.

30
00:02:00.948 --> 00:02:02.208
But you'll be better soon."

31
00:02:03.312 --> 00:02:07.394
Bessie went into the nearby housemaid's room, and I heard her talking.

32
00:02:07.414 --> 00:02:13.257
"Sarah, come sleep with me in the nursery," she said to the housemaid.

33
00:02:13.277 --> 00:02:17.138
"I don't dare be alone with that poor child tonight.

34
00:02:18.179 --> 00:02:19.039
She might die.

35
00:02:19.059 --> 00:02:23.001
It's so strange that she had a fit in the red room.

36
00:02:24.081 --> 00:02:25.982
Mrs. Reed was too hard on her."

37
00:02:27.143 --> 00:02:29.904
Sarah came back with her, and they both went to bed.

38
00:02:30.924 --> 00:02:33.907
They whispered for half an hour before they fell asleep.

39
00:02:35.068 --> 00:02:37.110
I caught scraps of their conversation.

40
00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:43.655
"Something passed her, all dressed in white, and then vanished Bessie murmured.

41
00:02:44.776 --> 00:02:50.862
At last they both slept, but I lay awake the whole night, still haunted by the ghost and

42
00:02:50.942 --> 00:02:52.303
Mrs. Reed's cruelty.

43
00:02:53.444 --> 00:02:57.908
By noon the next day, I sat wrapped in a shawl by the nursery hearth.

44
00:02:59.068 --> 00:03:04.710
I felt physically weak and broken down, but my worst ailment was a wretched sadness.

45
00:03:05.991 --> 00:03:10.233
No sooner had I wiped one tear from my cheek than another followed.

46
00:03:11.933 --> 00:03:13.354
Yet I should have been happy.

47
00:03:14.494 --> 00:03:18.756
None of the Reeds were there; they had all gone out in the carriage with their mama.

48
00:03:19.876 --> 00:03:22.137
Miss Abbot was sewing in another room.

49
00:03:23.298 --> 00:03:28.440
And Bessie addressed me kindly as she moved back and forth, putting away toys.

50
00:03:29.648 --> 00:03:35.651
Later Bessie went down to the kitchen and brought back a tart on a brightly painted plate that

51
00:03:35.691 --> 00:03:39.033
I'd always admired, yet had never been allowed to touch.

52
00:03:39.353 --> 00:03:43.536
But I was too miserable to eat the tart or enjoy the plate.

53
00:03:44.776 --> 00:03:50.019
She brought me a book and sang to me as she began making a bonnet for Georgiana's doll,

54
00:03:50.459 --> 00:03:51.460
but nothing helped.

55
00:03:52.601 --> 00:03:54.001
The next day Mr.

56
00:03:54.041 --> 00:03:54.902
Lloyd returned.

57
00:03:54.922 --> 00:03:57.563
"Well, nurse, how is she?"

58
00:03:57.924 --> 00:03:58.504
he asked.

59
00:03:59.652 --> 00:04:01.574
"Very well," replied Bessie.

60
00:04:01.594 --> 00:04:04.236
"Then she ought to look more cheerful.

61
00:04:05.257 --> 00:04:07.119
Why have you been crying, Miss Jane?

62
00:04:08.159 --> 00:04:09.080
Have you any pain?"

63
00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.542
"No, sir," I said.

64
00:04:11.562 --> 00:04:17.808
"I daresay she's crying because she couldn't go out in the carriage," said Bessie.

65
00:04:17.828 --> 00:04:20.990
"I never cried for such a thing in my life!"

66
00:04:21.431 --> 00:04:22.612
I said indignantly.

67
00:04:22.632 --> 00:04:25.494
"I cry because I'm miserable."

68
00:04:25.514 --> 00:04:27.256
"Oh, miss!"

69
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.542
Bessie said with a note of disapproval in her voice.

70
00:04:31.762 --> 00:04:34.164
Just then the bell rang for the servants' lunch.

71
00:04:35.324 --> 00:04:37.366
"That's for you, nurse," said Mr.

72
00:04:37.406 --> 00:04:41.228
Lloyd. "I'll stay with Miss Jane until you return."

73
00:04:42.288 --> 00:04:47.031
He waited for her to leave and then he said, "What made you ill yesterday?"

74
00:04:47.051 --> 00:04:50.773
"I was knocked down, but that didn't make me ill.

75
00:04:51.894 --> 00:04:55.176
I was shut up until after dark in a room where there's a ghost."

76
00:04:56.216 --> 00:04:57.457
"You're afraid of ghosts?"

77
00:04:57.477 --> 00:04:58.977
"Of Mr.

78
00:04:59.057 --> 00:04:59.878
Reed's ghost.

79
00:05:01.018 --> 00:05:02.259
He died in that room.

80
00:05:03.459 --> 00:05:06.300
It was cruel to shut me up alone without a candle."

81
00:05:08.021 --> 00:05:12.163
Mr. Lloyd could not understand why I was still miserable in the daylight.

82
00:05:13.183 --> 00:05:18.005
Finally I blurted out, "I have no father or mother, brothers or sisters."

83
00:05:18.025 --> 00:05:20.926
"You have a kind aunt and cousins."

84
00:05:20.946 --> 00:05:25.428
"John knocked me down, and my aunt shut me up in the red room."

85
00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:28.481
Mr. Lloyd took out his snuff box.

86
00:05:28.501 --> 00:05:32.562
"Aren't you thankful you have such a beautiful place to live?"

87
00:05:32.582 --> 00:05:35.043
"It's not my house, sir.

88
00:05:36.184 --> 00:05:38.805
If I had anywhere else to go, I'd be glad to leave."

89
00:05:38.825 --> 00:05:41.906
"Do you have any relatives besides Mrs.

90
00:05:41.986 --> 00:05:44.187
Reed?" I shook my head.

91
00:05:44.207 --> 00:05:48.689
"My aunt once said I might have some poor Eyre relations."

92
00:05:48.709 --> 00:05:52.370
"If we could find them, would you like to go to them?"

93
00:05:53.591 --> 00:05:54.571
I thought for a minute.

94
00:05:54.591 --> 00:05:58.318
"No, I shouldn't like to belong to poor people."

95
00:05:58.338 --> 00:06:01.420
"Would you like to go to school?"

96
00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:02.401
asked Mr.

97
00:06:02.441 --> 00:06:05.844
Lloyd. I scarcely knew what school was.

98
00:06:06.864 --> 00:06:12.169
John hated his school, but Bessie had worked in a house where the young ladies had prospered at

99
00:06:12.249 --> 00:06:17.373
school. She had boasted of the pictures they painted and the music they played.

100
00:06:18.594 --> 00:06:21.656
School implied an entrance into a new life.

101
00:06:21.676 --> 00:06:26.100
"I should indeed like to go to school," I finally announced.

102
00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:30.081
Soon I heard the carriage rolling up the gravel drive.

103
00:06:31.161 --> 00:06:32.882
Mr. Lloyd went to talk to Mrs.

104
00:06:32.962 --> 00:06:38.623
Reed, and because of what later happened, I assume he recommended I be sent to school.

105
00:06:39.824 --> 00:06:46.146
One night soon afterward, Bessie and Miss Abbot sat sewing in the nursery while they thought I slept.

106
00:06:47.286 --> 00:06:53.248
Miss Abbot talked about my family, and I learned for the first time that my father had been

107
00:06:53.288 --> 00:06:54.408
a poor clergyman.

108
00:06:56.200 --> 00:07:01.522
My mother's friends and family had considered him beneath her, but she married him anyway.

109
00:07:02.642 --> 00:07:08.584
Her father had cut her off without any money, and later my parents had died within a month

110
00:07:08.624 --> 00:07:09.144
of each other.

111
00:07:10.264 --> 00:07:12.765
When Miss Abbot finished, Bessie sighed.

112
00:07:13.825 --> 00:07:15.546
"Miss Jane should be pitied."

113
00:07:15.566 --> 00:07:22.768
"Yes, if she were a nice, pretty child, one might be compassionate, but one really cannot care for

114
00:07:22.808 --> 00:07:23.808
such a little toad."

115
00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:30.702
"A beauty like Miss Georgiana would be more moving in the same condition," agreed Bessie.

116
00:07:30.722 --> 00:07:34.503
"Oh, I dote on Miss Georgiana!"

117
00:07:34.823 --> 00:07:35.783
cried Miss Abbot.

118
00:07:36.843 --> 00:07:41.705
She paused for a moment and then said with equal fervor, "Shall we get some dinner?"

119
00:07:42.805 --> 00:07:47.326
And they went downstairs, leaving me with much to think about as I fell asleep.
